{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01D9L6ifwBLEhaqBh48jifUf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Second run APC 300:\n- STARK excl trace: 1114ms\n- MLE Rounds: 163ms\n- LogUp GKR: 366ms\n- Round 0: 181ms\n- WHIR: 127ms\n- Stacked Reduction: 73ms\n\nSo across two after-runs:\n- MLE Rounds: 161ms, 163ms → average 162ms\n- Before: 166ms\n- Change: -4ms average\n\nThis is right at the borderline of the 3ms rollback threshold. Let me think about this:\n\nBefore: MLE Rounds = 166ms\n\n\nAfter run 1: MLE Rounds = 161ms (-5ms)\nAfter run 2: MLE Rounds = 163ms (-3ms)\nAverage after: 162ms\nAverage improvement: 4ms\n\nThe rollback criteria says \"MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by less than 3ms\" → revert. We got 4ms average which is borderline but passes. For STARK excl trace, I'm seeing 1182ms before down to an average of 1118.5ms after, which is a 63.5ms improvement, though that's mostly driven by the second run.\n\nLooking at the WHIR component separately, it improved by about 66ms between runs, which is just noise unrelated to this change. The actual optimization gain from the MLE Rounds adjustment is around 4ms, which aligns with the expected 3-5ms range. So I need to evaluate whether this passes the rollback criteria: the MLE Rounds improvement of 4ms clears the 3ms threshold, but the STARK excl trace improvement appears to be mostly WHIR variance rather than a real signal from the optimization itself.\n\nConsidering the borderline results—4ms is technically above the 3ms threshold but still marginal—I think this warrants committing as a success. The implementation is sound, doesn't add unnecessary complexity, and shows consistent positive movement in MLE Rounds across both runs, so I'll move forward with writing the report and committing the change.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1490,"cache_rea